1
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:03,147
Jezero Crater:

2
00:00:03,197 --> 00:00:06,600
an ancient lake bed on Mars, where NASA's
Perseverance rover is searching for signs of

3
00:00:06,650 --> 00:00:10,053
past life.

4
00:00:10,103 --> 00:00:12,973
Now imagine planning your own walk there.

5
00:00:13,701 --> 00:00:15,454
We're Binary Explorers.

6
00:00:15,504 --> 00:00:20,156
Imam Hosen, our team lead, architected and
built the system.

7
00:00:20,206 --> 00:00:24,232
Ahad, our NASA data analyst, verified every
dataset.

8
00:00:24,282 --> 00:00:31,207
And Biswadev, our 3D designer and QA tester,
shaped the visuals and tested every feature.

9
00:00:31,935 --> 00:00:34,985
We love Mars, and we kept hitting the same
wall:

10
00:00:35,035 --> 00:00:39,633
the data is public, but the tools rarely show
where an answer came from.

11
00:00:39,683 --> 00:00:41,764
So we built one that always does.

12
00:00:43,492 --> 00:00:47,457
NASA publishes thirteen hundred Mars datasets,
elevation down to one metre, and the rover's

13
00:00:47,507 --> 00:00:51,472
path sol by sol.

14
00:00:51,522 --> 00:00:52,290
All open...

15
00:00:52,340 --> 00:00:53,331
but scattered.

16
00:00:54,059 --> 00:00:55,026
Who feels that?

17
00:00:55,076 --> 00:00:57,671
A student asking why Jezero was chosen.

18
00:00:57,721 --> 00:01:00,654
A teacher who wants a real mission in class.

19
00:01:00,704 --> 00:01:03,434
One day, the first crews to walk on Mars.

20
00:01:03,484 --> 00:01:07,570
And when a chatbot invents a coordinate, no
one can check it.

21
00:01:08,299 --> 00:01:11,244
Signals take up to twenty-two minutes between
Earth and Mars, so crews must plan on their

22
00:01:11,294 --> 00:01:14,239
own.

23
00:01:14,289 --> 00:01:15,259
The opportunity:

24
00:01:15,309 --> 00:01:19,082
NASA's evidence, and its limits, in one place
you can trace.

25
00:01:20,811 --> 00:01:21,711
Our big idea:

26
00:01:21,761 --> 00:01:26,718
Mars Explorer, a mission console where every
number is tied to a NASA source, starting with

27
00:01:26,768 --> 00:01:31,725
real HiRISE ground on NASA's elevation model.

28
00:01:32,453 --> 00:01:36,032
In the console, switch NASA Mars Trek layers,
and scrub sol by sol along Perseverance's

29
00:01:36,082 --> 00:01:39,661
published path.

30
00:01:40,389 --> 00:01:44,799
Official feature names, HiRISE terrain-model
footprints, and every NASA landing site come

31
00:01:44,849 --> 00:01:49,259
straight from data dot NASA dot gov.

32
00:01:49,987 --> 00:01:53,011
Load a route, and the system samples the
terrain:

33
00:01:53,061 --> 00:01:57,057
a Traverse Risk Index with every source, an
elevation and slope profile, and the named

34
00:01:57,107 --> 00:02:01,104
features and terrain models along the way.

35
00:02:01,832 --> 00:02:06,429
Pick two points for a slope-aware A* corridor,
always non-certifying, then fly it in 3D over

36
00:02:06,479 --> 00:02:11,076
HiRISE imagery.

37
00:02:11,804 --> 00:02:15,852
The assistant cites its passages, a command
palette puts every action one keystroke away,

38
00:02:15,902 --> 00:02:19,951
and one click makes a mission briefing.

39
00:02:20,679 --> 00:02:24,561
The mission agent routes each goal to the
right tools, runs them in parallel, and

40
00:02:24,611 --> 00:02:28,493
streams a grounded answer, step by step.

41
00:02:29,221 --> 00:02:33,459
Ask the corpus, and every sentence carries a
clickable citation.

42
00:02:33,509 --> 00:02:37,110
Retrieval previews live, new documents index
in seconds, and the embedding space shows how

43
00:02:37,160 --> 00:02:40,761
evidence is found.

44
00:02:41,489 --> 00:02:45,131
The Open Data page searches every Mars dataset
NASA lists, live or offline, with a data shelf

45
00:02:45,181 --> 00:02:48,823
for each mission.

46
00:02:49,551 --> 00:02:51,242
Earth gets its own lane:

47
00:02:51,292 --> 00:02:57,626
live EONET wildfires and storms, with full
provenance, never mixed into a Mars decision.

48
00:02:58,354 --> 00:03:01,024
Every page tells a 3D data story:

49
00:03:01,074 --> 00:03:07,412
Earth and Mars at true scale, Jezero cell by
cell, the traverse by sol, every NASA landing,

50
00:03:07,462 --> 00:03:13,800
slopes in relief, and a route raised into an
elevation curtain.

51
00:03:14,528 --> 00:03:15,261
Behind it:

52
00:03:15,311 --> 00:03:21,012
run traces, a live architecture view, a source
register, a gallery, model comparisons, Earth

53
00:03:21,062 --> 00:03:26,764
analogs, a guided story, and a shareable
mission card.

54
00:03:28,492 --> 00:03:29,273
What changes?

55
00:03:29,323 --> 00:03:33,366
Planning on Mars becomes something you can
check, classrooms get a real mission, and

56
00:03:33,416 --> 00:03:37,459
researchers reach the right archive faster.

57
00:03:38,187 --> 00:03:42,666
Next, we need planetary-science mentors,
support to sample HiRISE terrain models

58
00:03:42,716 --> 00:03:47,195
directly, and educators to pilot it with
students.

59
00:03:47,923 --> 00:03:51,316
One day, the first crew could rehearse their
Marswalk right here, with NASA's evidence at

60
00:03:51,366 --> 00:03:54,759
every step.

61
00:03:54,809 --> 00:03:55,850
Mars Explorer...

62
00:03:55,900 --> 00:03:57,350
from Binary Explorers.
